<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="1bbd4166">&lt;p&gt;
    The structure of the IT strategy document depends on the scope, level of effort and rigor applied for the strategy
    development activity. A well documented IT strategy document typically covers the following:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        IT imperatives and implications
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        IT challenges and constraints  
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        IT outcomes and measures of success
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        IT strategy statements and key performance indicators (KPIs)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        IT target capabilities
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        IT capability improvements, list of initiatives and KPIs
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        IT strategy articulation for branding and communication
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="b180e6c4"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="88dc3069"></attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="525f37fc"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="478f7317">&lt;p&gt;
    The purpose of this artifact is to:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Provide the direction, focus, plan and priorities for IT within an organization
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Explain the implications of business strategies and help align IT outcomes, strategy statements, capabilities
        and initiatives with the business goals, objectives and key outcomes.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
